in kuwait where the economy is worsening  as a result of low oil prices and the  coronavirus pandemic  parliamentary elections still went ahead  as planned  voters masked up before casting their  ballots and said they hoped these polls  would begin to turn things around  i boycotted the past two elections this  time i thought that i should participate  because the situation is deteriorating  we need to be united  and elect reformists in the parliament  so that the situation may improve  hopefully  i hope that the kuwaiti people will  choose wisely that they will choose the  people who represent kuwait  change is a must these are the first  elections in kuwait  since its new emir took office in  september  after the death of his half-brother  with the opposition weakened in recent  years analysts say no major political  shift  is expected and with the campaign season  severely muted due to the pandemic  many believe voter turnout will be lower  this year  up for grabs are 50 seats in parliament  and among the 326 candidates  a record number of women 29 are running  we have to fight corruption by choosing  who will represent us inside the  national assembly  and be a person with conscience honesty  who loves this country and its citizens  kuwait's nearly 140 billion economy is  facing a deficit of 46 billion dollars  this year  in september it was downgraded for the  first time by moody's investors service  and just a month earlier kuwait's  finance minister warned the government  may no longer be able to pay  salaries still analysts say it's not  just the economy that will pose a  challenge to the upcoming parliament  also the lack of trust and confidence  between  people among people with the parliament  and government they need them to be more  uh  effective than than what used to be and  i think this is going to be  seen in in hopefully in the election  results  many say that no matter the results the  biggest difficulty going forward  may be how to overcome legislative  gridlock mohammed al jazeera
 

